        "committer": {
          "name": "Andreas Dilger",
          "email": "adilger@thelustrecollective.com",
          "date": "2026-11-04 08:16:33.000000000",
          "tz": -360
        },
        "subject": "LU-20690 ec: no raid set with a single data stripe",
        "message": "LU-20690 ec: no raid set with a single data stripe\n\nec_split_stripes() split an odd number of data stripes with k=2\ninto raid sets of 2 and one set with a single data stripe, e.g.\n{2, 2, 1} for 5 stripes.  The parity of that set is only a copy\nof its one data stripe, but it needs as many parity stripes as\na full raid set, and the extra set made layouts such as\n\"-c 5 --ec 2+2\" fail the check that the parity stripes fit in\nthe data stripe count.\n\nUse one raid set fewer in this case, so the residual stripe goes\ninto the first raid set, {3, 2} for 5 stripes and {3, 2, 2} for 7.\nk=2 with an odd total of 5 or more stripes is the only case that\nmade a single-stripe raid set, other splits are unchanged.  All\nusers of the split size their buffers from esc_k0 rather than k,\nas a 3-stripe file with k=2 already gives one raid set of 3.\n\nThe split is computed from the layout rather than stored in it,\nso it has to change before EC layouts are in use.\n\nlod_ec_shrink_data_geometry() reduced the split one data stripe at\na time when fewer OSTs were available, which could still leave a\nsingle-stripe raid set, e.g. {2, 2, 1} for 5 of 6 stripes, unlike\nthe {3, 2} that clients compute from the layout.  Use\nec_split_stripes() for the reduced stripe count instead.\n\nAdd sanity-ec test_31h to check the new raid sets with --ec-map\nand that \"-c 5 --ec 2+2\" works, and test_65e for recovery of a\n3-stripe raid set.\n\nTest-Parameters: trivial testlist=sanity-ec\nSigned-off-by: Andreas Dilger <adilger@thelustrecollective.com>\nAssisted-by: ClaudeCode:claude-opus-5-5 ltvm llm_code_and_review_tools\nChange-Id: I389da5b297651119989be724c1e84868ea3ebbe5\n"
      },
      "branch": "refs/heads/master"
    }
